# Global Development Rules

## Core principles
- Prefer the simplest solution that fully satisfies the requested behavior.
- Match the established patterns, architecture, naming, and formatting of the repository before introducing a new pattern.
- Keep changes limited to the requested scope.
- Do not refactor, rename, reformat, upgrade, or improve unrelated code.
- Do not create abstractions unless they reduce real, measurable complexity.
- Prefer clear duplication over premature abstraction.
- Use strict TypeScript.
- Do not use `any` unless there is no safe alternative.
- Prefer named exports unless the framework or repository convention requires a default export.

## Existing code takes priority
- When editing an existing file, preserve its local conventions.
- Do not convert callbacks to async/await, classes to hooks, or one state-management approach to another unless explicitly requested.
- For new React code, use functional components and hooks by default.
- Do not rewrite a file only to conform to a newer preference.

## Naming
- Use PascalCase for React components, classes, types, interfaces, and enums.
- Use camelCase for functions, variables, object properties, and instances.
- Use SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE for environment variables and true constants.
- Use kebab-case for folders and non-component file names unless the repository uses a different convention.

## Comments
- Do not add comments by default.
- Add comments only for non-obvious intent, business constraints, security decisions, browser or device workarounds, or external-system behavior.
- Never comment what the code obviously does.
- Never mention tickets, tasks, AI generation, temporary fixes, or implementation history in source comments.

## Dependencies
- Check existing dependencies before suggesting or adding a package.
- Do not add a dependency when existing project tooling or a few maintainable lines of code solve the problem.
- Do not introduce duplicate libraries for state, forms, validation, styling, routing, dates, or UI.
- Request approval before adding, removing, upgrading, or changing production dependencies.

## Completion communication
- When work is complete, summarize what changed and why in 2–3 sentences.
- State which verification commands were run and whether they passed.
- Clearly mention unexpected findings, known limitations, skipped checks, or judgment calls.
- Never claim work is complete if required verification failed or was not run.